From my own knowledge: To blink an LED without blocking the program (using `delay()`), you must use the `millis()` function. This function returns the number of milliseconds since the board started. You store the time when the LED state last changed and compare it to the current time in every loop iteration. If enough time has passed, you toggle the LED and update the stored time.

Here is the Arduino sketch for pin 13:

MAKE CODE: Non-blocking Blink
language arduino
/*
 * Blink without delay()
 * Toggles LED on pin 13 every 500ms using millis().
 */
const int ledPin = 13;
const unsigned long interval = 500; // 500 milliseconds

unsigned long lastChangeTime = 0;
bool ledState = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  // Optional: set initial state
  digitalWrite(ledPin, ledState ? HIGH : LOW);
}

void loop() {
  unsigned long currentTime = millis();

  // Check if enough time has passed since the last change
  if (currentTime - lastChangeTime >= interval) {
    // Toggle the LED state
    ledState = !ledState;
    digitalWrite(ledPin, ledState ? HIGH : LOW);

    // Update the timestamp
    lastChangeTime = currentTime;
  }

  // Other code can run here without stopping the blink
}
END
